.TH EVENT_LOOP_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
event_loop_declare_scoped \- declare an event_loop with automatic destruction on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

event_loop_declare_scoped(name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an event_loop variable initialised to NULL and tagged with a
GCC/Clang cleanup attribute (via _ccol_destructor) that calls
___event_loop_destroy() automatically when the variable goes out of
scope.  The variable must still be assigned via event_loop_construct()
or an event_loop_create*() call before use.

Prefer event_loop_construct_scoped() when declaration and
initialisation can happen together, which is the common case.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  Variable name to declare.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Safe to leave the variable NULL through the whole scope (the cleanup
function is a no-op on NULL).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
{
    event_loop_declare_scoped(loop);
    char *err = NULL;
    loop = event_loop_create(32, 1, 1, &err);
    if (!loop) { fprintf(stderr, "%s\en", err); return; }
    /* ... */
}  /* loop is automatically destroyed here */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR event_loop_declare (3),
.BR event_loop_construct_scoped (3),
.BR event_loop_create (3)
